{"name":"Chantilly Lace","hex":"#f1e2de","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"lace","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A light fabric containing patterns of holes, usually built up from a single thread.","A cord or ribbon passed through eyelets in a shoe or garment, pulled tight and tied to fasten the shoe or garment firmly."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To fasten (something) with laces.","To interweave items."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/lace"}],"entries":[{"title":"Chantilly lace","blocks":[{"text":"Chantilly lace is a handmade bobbin lace named after the city of Chantilly, France, in a tradition dating from the 17th century. The famous silk laces were introduced in the 18th century. Chantilly lace, was also produced in the 19th century but this one was actually made not in Chantilly area but in the French Norman town Bayeux and in Geraardsbergen, now in Belgium."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/Sjaal_in_Chantilly_kloskant%2C_1850-1880.jpg/330px-Sjaal_in_Chantilly_kloskant%2C_1850-1880.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chantilly_lace"},{"title":"Chantilly Lace (song)","blocks":[{"text":"\"Chantilly Lace\" is a 1958 rock and roll song by The Big Bopper. It was produced by Jerry Kennedy, and reached No. 6 on the US Billboard Hot 100. Bruce Channel covered the song on his 1962 album, Hey! Baby. The song was also covered by Jerry Lee Lewis in 1972."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/5/55/Chantilly_Lace_%28song%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chantilly_Lace_(song)"},{"title":"Lace","blocks":[{"text":"Lace is a decorative openwork textile made without the use of pre-existing fabric. Lace can be made by hand or machine. Various techniques can be employed to form lace, each having a distinct category. Lace can also be used to refer to other types of openwork fabric such as cutwork or filet, which use pre-existing fabric."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/Verknipte_kantstrook.JPG/330px-Verknipte_kantstrook.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lace"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/Detail_van_sjerp_in_Chantilly_kloskant%2C_1850-1880.jpg/500px-Detail_van_sjerp_in_Chantilly_kloskant%2C_1850-1880.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/Detail_van_sjerp_in_Chantilly_kloskant%2C_1850-1880.jpg/500px-Detail_van_sjerp_in_Chantilly_kloskant%2C_1850-1880.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Scarf in Chantilly lace - MoMu-collection, Antwerp (Detail)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADetail_van_sjerp_in_Chantilly_kloskant%2C_1850-1880.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/Mitaines_in_Chantilly_kloskant%2C_1850-1890.jpg/500px-Mitaines_in_Chantilly_kloskant%2C_1850-1890.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/Mitaines_in_Chantilly_kloskant%2C_1850-1890.jpg/500px-Mitaines_in_Chantilly_kloskant%2C_1850-1890.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Mitts in Chantilly lace - MoMu-collection, Antwerp","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMitaines_in_Chantilly_kloskant%2C_1850-1890.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/CUR.55.96.123b_view21.jpg/500px-CUR.55.96.123b_view21.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/CUR.55.96.123b_view21.jpg/1280px-CUR.55.96.123b_vi